# ☁️ AWS Notes

---

## 🖥️ EC2 — Elastic Cloud Compute

An AWS EC2 instance is a virtual server in the cloud that you can rent from Amazon Web Services (AWS) to run your applications and workloads.

| 🔑 Term | 📖 Meaning |
|--------|-----------|
| ⚡ **Elastic** | Multiple service providers and its prefix. Whenever AWS provide service that scaled up and scaled down that means this service elastic in nature. |
| 🌐 **Cloud** | Public platform |
| 💻 **Compute** | CPU, RAM, Disk, virtual server |

---

### ✅ What is the advantage of EC2 instance?

- 🔒 Deliver secure, reliable, high-performance, and cost-effective compute infrastructure to meet demanding business needs.
- ⚙️ Access the on-demand infrastructure and capacity you need to run HPC applications faster and cost-effectively.
- ⏱️ Access environments in minutes, dynamically scale capacity as needed, and benefit from AWS's pay-as-you-go pricing.
- 🤖 Deliver the broadest choice of compute, networking (up to 400 Gbps), and storage services purpose-built to optimize price performance for ML projects.

---

### 📦 What are the different types of EC2 instance?

Depending on application we can choose instance type.

- 🔧 General purpose
- ⚡ Compute optimized
- 🧠 Memory optimized
- 💾 Storage optimized
- 🚀 Accelerated computing

---

### 🔐 EC2 Access

- 🗝️ To access instance, you need a key and keypair Name
- ⬇️ You can download the private key only once
- 🔄 The public key is saved by AWS to match it to the keypair name, and private key when you try to log in to the EC2 instance.
- 🚫 Without keypair you cannot access instance via RDP or SSH (Linux)
- 📊 There is 20 EC2 instance soft limit per region, you can submit request to AWS to increase limit

---

### 🩺 EC2 Status Check

- ⏱️ By default, AWS EC2 service performs automatically status check every one minute.
- 🔍 This is done on every running EC2 instance to identify any H/W or software issue
- 🔧 Status check is built into the AWS EC2 instance.
- 🚫 They cannot be configured, deleted or disabled.
- 📈 EC2 Service can send its metric Data to AWS CloudWatch every 5 min

---

## 🚀 Create EC2 Instance and Deploy Jenkins on AWS

- 🔎 Search EC2 instance and click
- 🖱️ Click on instance running
- ➡️ Then your right side you can see launch instance menu, click on same.
- 📝 Please choose name of your instance.
- 🐧 Choose OS of your instance. Here I can choose Ubuntu OS. Again, AWS says which version do you want. Make sure you can choose free tier eligible resources. Whenever you can choose free tier, AWS provide 750 hrs t2 micro. If you can choose any other than t2 micro then AWS will charge you.
<img src="aws-console.png" alt="AWS Console">
> 🎁 **Free tier:** In your first year includes 750 hours of t2.micro (or t3.micro in the Regions in which t2.micro is unavailable) instance usage on free tier AMIs per month, 750 hours of public IPv4 address usage per month, 30 GiB of EBS storage, 2 million IOs, 1 GB of snapshots, and 100 GB of bandwidth to the internet.

- 🔑 Key pair is required, it nothing but combination of public key and private key.
- ➕ Create new keypair, provide name and select RSA.
- 🌐 Keep network setting as it is, we will not touch for this lab.
- 🖱️ Then you need to click on launch instance and below screen will appear.
- ✅ Once instance created then access instance through Putty.
- 🔄 You must update the packages on OS.

> 🐧 If you are ubuntu user then use this command:
> ```bash
> sudo apt update
> ```

> 👑 If you are root user then use this command:
> ```bash
> apt update
> ```

- ☕ Then we need to deploy Jenkins on ubuntu. For that there is prerequisite to install JDK.

```bash
sudo apt install openjdk-17-jre-headless
```

> 💡 This command will prompt you to confirm the installation by typing 'y' (yes) and then press Enter. It will download and install the OpenJDK 17 JRE along with any necessary dependencies.

- ✅ Once the installation is complete, you can verify that Java has been installed correctly by checking the version:

```bash
java -version
```

- 🔗 Then Finally we will go ahead and install Jenkins using Jenkins official URL:
  👉 https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/installing/linux/

- 📦 Select weekly release plugin, if you want to download then use below code for same:

```bash
sudo wget -O /usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc \
https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian/jenkins.io-2023.key

echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc]" \
https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian binary/ | sudo tee \
/etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install Jenkins
```

- ⚙️ Once we paste above command in putty then Jenkins will start installing on our ubuntu instance.

- 🔍 Once Jenkins installed then verify whether Jenkins service running or not:

```bash
systemctl status Jenkins
```

> 📋 This command will display the status of the Jenkins service, including whether it is running, stopped, or encountering any issues.

**🔎 Interpreting the Output:**

| 🏷️ Field | 📖 Description |
|---------|--------------|
| ✅ **Active** | Indicates whether the service is currently running `active (running)`, stopped `inactive (dead)`, or encountering an issue `failed` |
| 🔢 **Main PID** | Shows the process ID of the main Jenkins process |
| 📁 **Loaded** | Indicates whether the service unit file is loaded and whether it is enabled to start on boot |

- 🌐 Once everything is done then we will have to access public IP using **http port number 8080** to verify whether Jenkins is installed or not and we can get Jenkins page on browsers.

---

### ⚠️ Permission Denied Error — Troubleshooting

> ❌ Error: `cat: /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword: Permission denied`

The error message indicates that the user attempting to read the initialAdminPassword file does not have the necessary permissions to do so.

**🔧 Here's how you can troubleshoot and resolve this issue:**

**1️⃣ Check Permissions** — First, verify the permissions of the file initialAdminPassword using the `ls -l` command:

```bash
ls -l /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
```

**2️⃣ Verify User Permissions** — Ensure that the user running the cat command has read permissions on the file. Typically, Jenkins creates this file with restricted permissions for security reasons.

**3️⃣ Use `sudo`** — If you have administrative privileges, you can use sudo to run the cat command with elevated permissions:

```bash
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
```

- 🔓 Then retrieve password and paste in the Jenkins unlock screen.

> 🎉 **Congratulations!** We have successfully deployed our first application on ubuntu instance.

